#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110299 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "854edbd5-3aef-5c6b-bb79-967aa241beb2" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Rules of Exponents Without Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "ee3fd0ee-77a9-5ebb-9bc9-c7439f5946b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1088" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\left(x^2y\\right)^3\\cdot x^2y$." :question/worked-solution "We start by using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n(x^{2}y)^{3} \\cdot x^{2}y =; (x^{2})^{3} \\cdot y^{3} \\cdot x^{2}y\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2 \\cdot 3} \\cdot y^{3} \\cdot x^{2}y &=  \\\\\n&= x^{6} \\cdot y^{3} \\cdot x^{2}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{6} \\cdot y^{3} \\cdot x^{2}y = \\\\\n&= x^{6} \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y^{3} \\cdot y = \\\\\nx^{6 + 2} \\cdot y^{3 + 1} &=  \\\\\nx^{8} \\cdot y^{4} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{8}y^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110300 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110662 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "736c1b75-fe60-5f0a-b426-a19f3edaaa2b" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Rules of Exponents With Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "d8aca072-8ed5-5d49-8d4e-8bcc96a27ddb" :question/math-academy-id "e-1089" :question/problem "Given that $a=2x^3$ and $b=\\dfrac{x}{y}$, express $ab^2$ in terms of $x$ and $y$." :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute for $a$ and $b$:\n\n$$\nab^{2} = 2x^{3}(\\frac{x}{y})^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n2x^{3}(\\frac{x}{y})^{2} = 2x^{3}(\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}})\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply and apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{3}(\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2x^{3} \\cdot x^{2}}{y^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2x^{3 + 2}}{y^{2}} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x^{5}}{y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110301 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "014ea973-359e-53f5-9151-adc85e08bd3f" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Rules of Exponents With Radicals"}] :question/id #uuid "cf539049-76f2-56c0-a890-00c2d767c0a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-1091" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac {\\sqrt{y}} {\\sqrt[3]{y}}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the radicals as fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} = \\frac{y^{1/2}}{y^{1/3}}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{1/2}}{y^{1/3}} &=  \\\\\ny^{1/2 - 1/3} &=  \\\\\ny^{3/6 - 2/6} &=  \\\\\n&= y^{1/6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\ny^{1/6} = \\sqrt[6]{y}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "854edbd5-3aef-5c6b-bb79-967aa241beb2" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Rules of Exponents Without Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0e4b119-fd88-5056-850b-f6d795687b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{4}y^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107382} {:answer/id #uuid "421753ae-7e6c-575e-8877-57f2fc9608b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}y^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107383} {:answer/id #uuid "51415358-19cd-5403-96f5-38ef07884fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107384} {:answer/id #uuid "7cac2597-4dda-5361-a4b1-aef545b1852f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107385} {:answer/id #uuid "7db577b1-76eb-51ca-b024-1fc85e6fc9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107386}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc75e3b